# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
#
# Key layout for the Lenovo Keyboard Pack For Yoga Tab (17ef:62b2, bus 0x0019).
# Covers both evdev nodes the folio exposes: the keyboard and the touchpad.
#
# Derived from the stock ZUI layout, which is AOSP's Generic.kl plus a Lenovo
# function row. Fourteen of Lenovo's entries used private keycode labels that
# AOSP does not define. That matters more than it looks: KeyLayoutMap returns
# BAD_VALUE on an unknown label and abandons the *whole file*, so shipping the
# stock layout unchanged would leave the keyboard with no layout at all.
#
# Remapped to their nearest AOSP equivalent:
#   LENOVO_VOLUME_MUTE -> VOLUME_MUTE     SCREEN_LOCK   -> LOCK
#   GLOBAL_SEARCH      -> SEARCH          FULL_SCREEN   -> FULLSCREEN
#   ZUI_SETTING        -> SETTINGS        SPLIT_SCREEN  -> RECENT_APPS
#   APP1               -> F19             APP2          -> F20
#
# ★★ APP1/APP2 were MACRO_1/MACRO_2 until session 16, and that was a dead end.
# PhoneWindowManager.java:5124-5129 clears ACTION_PASS_TO_USER for MACRO_1..4 in
# interceptKeyBeforeQueueing; InputDispatcher.cpp:1860-1873 then skips the whole
# interceptKeyBeforeDispatching stage, which is the only place custom input
# gestures are evaluated (KeyGestureController.java:1057-1070). A MACRO key can
# therefore never reach a user-configurable action — the two keys were
# structurally unbindable, not merely unbound.
#
# F19/F20 survive that gate: PhoneWindowManager.java:5130-5148 strips F13..F24
# only `if (!enableNew25q2Keycodes())`, and that flag is ENABLED in both release
# configs this tree builds. Confirmed on the running device rather than
# inferred:  `adb shell device_config list input` reports
#   com.android.hardware.input.enable_new_25q2_keycodes=true
#   com.android.hardware.input.enable_customizable_input_gestures=true
# The full derivation, including the three keycode families that were rejected,
# is in keylayout/Vendor_17ef_Product_617f.kl — it is the same argument and is
# not repeated here.
#
# What the keys DO is not decided here. MalbecParts binds them at runtime with
# InputManager#addCustomInputGesture (per-user, persisted by the framework in
# /data/system/users/<id>/input_gestures.xml), and exposes them under
# Settings > System > Keyboard > Physical keyboard > Advanced keyboard settings.
#
# ⚠️ Custom input gestures are keyed on (keycode, modifierState) ONLY — NOT per
# device (InputGestureManager.getCustomGestureForKeyEvent, :499-513). The F-key
# range is therefore allocated across BOTH input devices, and the two files must
# stay disjoint:
#     F13..F16, F18  Bluetooth pen (Vendor_17ef_Product_617f.kl)
#     F17            this keyboard, touchpad key (F7 keycap)
#     F19, F20       this keyboard, APP1 / APP2
#     F21            this keyboard, split-screen key (F10 keycap)
#     F22            this keyboard, PC-link key (F11 keycap)
#     F23            this keyboard, Fn + APP1 (search)
#     F24            this keyboard, full-screen key (F9 keycap) — see :608
#   (⚠️ this list said "F21..F24 unallocated" for four sessions after they were
#    allocated. Nothing free remains.)
#
# Stock says exactly three keys on this folio are user-customisable — APP1, APP2
# and an "AI" key — from work/unpacked/parts/system/usr/kb-type-config/
# lenovo_keyboard.xml (`support_diy_app1/app2/ai = true`; everything else in the
# function row is fixed). The AI key has no usage in stock's own keylayout, so
# it is not mapped here; if `getevent -lt` ever shows one, it gets F21.
#
# ★★ 655 -> SLEEP works. 654 -> WAKEUP CANNOT, and no keylayout change can fix
# that. This was measured on THIS build with the owner working the cover, three
# channels on one timeline (work/s13/folio.log; GPIO 135 is the cover magnet's
# hall switch, read straight out of /sys/kernel/debug/gpio, so it is independent
# of every layer of software):
#
#     t= 0.00  gpio135=HIGH  Awake     cover open, screen on
#     t=34.83  gpio135=LOW   Awake     COVER CLOSED
#     t=34.84  event8 028f (655) DOWN  keyboard reports it 10 ms later
#     t=35.12  gpio135=LOW   Dozing    screen off  <- 655 -> SLEEP did this
#     t=51.32  gpio135=HIGH  Dozing    COVER OPENED - and NO event at all
#     t=68.96  gpio135=LOW   Dozing    closed again - still no event
#     t=88.95  gpio135=HIGH  Dozing    opened again - still no event
#
# So: while the display is off the keyboard node is completely silent. Not just
# 654 - the second close does not even produce a 655. The MCU gates the UART on
# the display/suspend state, so an input event can never be what wakes this
# device. (The device was not even deep-suspended during that capture: the probe
# script was polling at 5 Hz. It still delivered nothing.)
#
# Both lines are kept anyway:
#   - 655 SLEEP is the mechanism that demonstrably closes the lid today. It is
#     also the fast one - it lands ~300 ms before the sensor path could.
#   - 654 WAKEUP WAKE is harmless. It has never been observed to fire, but if a
#     future keyboard firmware does emit it while the device is awake, waking is
#     the right response. The WAKE flag is required for POLICY_FLAG_WAKE
#     regardless.
#
# The wake half is done in configs/device_state_configuration.xml, off the
# BU52053NVX hall sensor, which is a wakeup sensor on the sensor DSP and is the
# only thing in the system that still reports while the AP is asleep. That file
# has the full derivation.
#
# ⚠️ An earlier revision of this comment claimed 655 also fires when the
# keyboard is folded back to a stand angle, citing
# work/stock-harvest/20260804-012130/getevent-folio.txt. That reading was wrong
# on both counts: the owner has since confirmed this folio has no stand
# position - it is fully open or fully closed, nothing between - so the "step 3"
# events in that capture were just a mistimed close/open pair.
#
# Stock's own layout for this VID/PID:
#     work/unpacked/parts/system/usr/keylayout/Vendor_17ef_Product_62b2.kl:457
#     key 654   KEYBOARD_RESTORE
#     key 655   KEYBOARD_REVERSE
# Emitted by the keyboard MCU over UART through lenovo_kb_analyze ->
# lenovo_kb_cuskey_report.
#
# The cover magnet is a completely separate part: a ROHM BU52053NVX hall switch
# on GPIO 135, owned by the sensor DSP and visible only as an Android Sensor
# (`qti.sensor.hall_effect`). Nothing about it reaches /dev/input. It is handled
# in configs/device_state_configuration.xml, which is the AOSP-supported way to
# get close-to-sleep / open-to-wake without patching the framework; that file
# documents the evidence, including how the polarity was measured.
#
# Dropped, with no sensible AOSP equivalent:
#   FN_LOCK           handled inside the keyboard firmware
#   TP_MUTE           AOSP has no touchpad toggle keycode
#   SUPER_CONNECT     Lenovo device linking
#   FN_Q              Lenovo private
#
